      "sample_index": 7,
      "text": "To find the mean value of the measures of the three interior angles of any triangle, we need to first recall the basic property of triangles which states that the sum of the interior angles of any triangle is always equal to 180 degrees.\n\nLet's denote the measures of the three interior angles of a triangle as A, B, and C. According to the property of triangles, we have:\n\nA + B + C = 180 degrees\n\nNow, to find the mean value of the measures of the three interior angles, we need to divide the sum of the angles by the total number of angles, which is 3 in this case:\n\nMean value = (A + B + C) / 3\n\nSubstituting the value of A + B + C from the property of triangles, we get:\n\nMean value = 180 degrees / 3\n\nMean value = 60 degrees\n\nTherefore, the mean value of the measures of the three interior angles of any triangle is 60 degrees.",
